@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# Moving the installed app to a new origin
+
+Old: `https://dailygrind.example.com` → New: `https://app.thedailygrind.example`
+(set in `migration.config.json`; run `npm run build:migration` after editing).
+
+## How it works
+
+**App identity & OS integration** — handled by the browser using the
+[PWA origin migration](https://github.com/WICG/manifest-incubations) manifest fields:
+
+| Origin | Manifest | Key field |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| old | `/legacy.webmanifest` | `migrate_to: { id, install_url }` |
+| new | `/manifest.webmanifest` | `migrate_from: [{ id, behavior: "suggest" }]` |
+
+Both sides point at each other (mutual consent), and the new origin also
+publishes `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association`. `index.html` picks the
+manifest at runtime from `location.origin`, so the same files deploy to both
+hosts. Name, icons, display mode and the `shortcuts` list (Order, Menu, Rewards,
+Locations) are identical in both manifests, so pinned icons, jump-list/dock
+shortcuts and window settings come over unchanged. Set `behavior` to `"force"`
+to migrate without asking the user.
+
+**User preferences** — localStorage is per-origin, so `js/migration.js` moves it
+with a one-time top-level handoff:
+
+1. First launch on the new origin with no saved prefs → checks that the old origin
+   is reachable, then goes to `OLD/migrate-export.html?return=<path>`.
+2. The old origin reads its prefs and redirects to `NEW<path>#dg-migrate=<payload>`
+   (the fragment is never sent to a server; the destination origin is hard-coded).
+3. The new origin checks the payload against the settings schema, merges it
+   (anything already saved on the new origin wins), records `dg:migration`, and
+   removes the fragment from the URL.
+
+It runs at most once. If the old origin can't be reached, it's skipped instead of
+leaving the user stuck. Users still on the old origin see a "new home" banner that
+starts the same handoff.
+
+## Deploy checklist
+
+1. Deploy this build to **both** origins (the old origin must keep serving
+   `legacy.webmanifest`, `migrate-export.html` and `js/` — don't 301 it yet).
+2. Serve `*.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json` and
+   `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json`.
+3. Keep the old origin up until migration traffic drops off. After that it can
+   redirect everything, and new-origin users who haven't migrated yet just start
+   with default preferences.
